1
00:00:00,590 --> 00:00:06,590
Hello and welcome to this massive open online course introduction to virus management.

2
00:00:06,590 --> 00:00:13,590
I am your instructor Kenneth and in this module we will be focusing on the management of clinical samples.

3
00:00:13,590 --> 00:00:28,380
Clinical samples are samples derived from hospital patients and these are analyzed routinely in hospital laboratories

4
00:00:28,380 --> 00:00:32,380
or in specifically designated pathological laboratories.

5
00:00:32,380 --> 00:00:40,340
The objectives of this module are to introduce you to the different types of clinical samples,

6
00:00:40,340 --> 00:00:45,340
to understand the various processes involved in analyzing clinical samples,

7
00:00:45,340 --> 00:00:50,340
to assess the risks posed during the processing of clinical samples,

8
00:00:50,340 --> 00:00:54,340
to identify pertinent controls for the mitigation of the risks

9
00:00:54,340 --> 00:00:59,340
and to understand the implications for biosecurity management of clinical samples.

10
00:00:59,340 --> 00:01:04,840
The learning outcomes for this module are as follows.

11
00:01:04,840 --> 00:01:12,840
Upon completion of this module you should be able to describe the various types of clinical samples,

12
00:01:12,840 --> 00:01:19,840
describe the process involved in the diagnosis of microbial, serological and nucleic acid samples,

13
00:01:19,840 --> 00:01:26,840
apply the pertinent controls to mitigate the risks posed by processing of clinical samples

14
00:01:26,840 --> 00:01:36,840
and describe the management of the security aspects of sample analysis.

15
00:01:36,840 --> 00:01:40,840
Clinical samples can present themselves in many ways.

16
00:01:40,840 --> 00:01:51,840
They can be in the form of blood, serum, swabs, stool, urine, body fluids or biopsies.

17
00:01:51,840 --> 00:02:03,520
Risk assessment for clinical samples has to take the following facts into consideration.

18
00:02:03,520 --> 00:02:11,520
The first fact is that the clinical sample consists of multiple biological agents derived from a single patient.

19
00:02:11,520 --> 00:02:16,520
In this case, the risk group will be unknown.

20
00:02:16,520 --> 00:02:20,520
And it is advisable to assume the highest risk group.

21
00:02:20,520 --> 00:02:30,630
One of the ways in which you can obtain information about the sample is by referring to the sample collection sheet.

22
00:02:30,630 --> 00:02:39,630
This will offer an insight into the case history of the patient as well as his or her current status.

23
00:02:39,630 --> 00:02:50,740
Take note that some of the patients may be asymptomatic carriers and may not manifest any of the clinical symptoms

24
00:02:50,740 --> 00:02:56,740
which are associated with a specific biological agent.

25
00:02:56,740 --> 00:03:06,740
In some cases, the sample may be transported or dissolved in a specific chemical or reagent.

26
00:03:06,740 --> 00:03:13,740
In this case, we must refer to the material safety data sheets which will accompany the samples.

27
00:03:13,740 --> 00:03:20,220
Sample processing.

28
00:03:20,220 --> 00:03:26,220
There are four diagnostic procedures which can be associated with the processing of clinical isolates.

29
00:03:26,220 --> 00:03:32,220
The first procedure may involve microbial isolation and culture.

30
00:03:32,220 --> 00:03:38,220
In this case, the laboratory technician will perform a serial dilution of the sample

31
00:03:38,220 --> 00:03:50,220
and culture the biological agent on a selective medium or isolate DNA directly from the sample itself.

32
00:03:53,080 --> 00:04:00,080
The second diagnostic procedure involves serological tests such as enzyme-linked immunosorbent assays

33
00:04:00,080 --> 00:04:06,080
in order to determine the interaction between antigen and antibodies.

34
00:04:06,080 --> 00:04:13,080
The third procedure may involve nucleic acid extraction and polymerase chain reaction

35
00:04:13,080 --> 00:04:20,080
in order to analyze viral DNA and RNA from patient samples.

36
00:04:20,080 --> 00:04:30,880
And the fourth procedure may involve microscopy of the sample.

37
00:04:30,880 --> 00:04:39,880
Risk mitigation is generally applied to mitigate or reduce the risk caused by the biological agent.

38
00:04:39,880 --> 00:04:45,880
The first step in risk mitigation involves the transport chain.

39
00:04:45,880 --> 00:04:51,880
The sample will generally be transported from the hospital or collection center via the transport network.

40
00:04:51,880 --> 00:04:58,220
You must ensure that the sample is packaged appropriately.

41
00:04:58,220 --> 00:05:01,220
The sample is clearly labeled.

42
00:05:01,220 --> 00:05:11,980
Absorbent material is included in the sample packaging to absorb any of the spills which may occur during transit.

43
00:05:11,980 --> 00:05:20,980
Adequate cooling agents such as dry ice or ice packs must be shipped with the sample in order to maintain its integrity.

44
00:05:20,980 --> 00:05:28,980
The usage of ice flakes is not recommended as this may be prone to contamination.

45
00:05:28,980 --> 00:05:33,980
Personnel such as drivers and couriers must be trained in sample transport

46
00:05:33,980 --> 00:05:40,980
and must be aware of the risk caused by the biological agent contained within the sample.

47
00:05:40,980 --> 00:05:50,130
This is one of the symbols which are generally labeled onto samples.

48
00:05:50,130 --> 00:05:53,130
This is the symbol for biohazard.

49
00:05:53,130 --> 00:06:01,670
Risk mitigation and sample documentation.

50
00:06:01,670 --> 00:06:07,670
All samples must be documented upon receipt in both an electronic and handwritten format.

51
00:06:07,670 --> 00:06:13,670
Samples may be assigned specific codes in order to protect the identity of the patient.

52
00:06:13,670 --> 00:06:18,670
Documents associated with the sample must be stored in secure locations

53
00:06:18,670 --> 00:06:24,670
or if it is electronic data, it must be stored on secure servers.

54
00:06:24,670 --> 00:06:30,670
Inventory management is essential in the event that the samples are archived.

55
00:06:30,670 --> 00:06:38,030
Risk mitigation for sample culture.

56
00:06:38,030 --> 00:06:44,030
Microbial samples derived from bacteria viruses may be cultured in appropriate media.

57
00:06:44,030 --> 00:06:51,030
Risk assessment must be conducted for exonic cultures based on the specific risk group.

58
00:06:51,030 --> 00:07:00,030
Some samples may have specific risks associated with them such as the ability to produce pores or be dispersed via aerosols.

59
00:07:00,030 --> 00:07:06,030
The judicious use of controls is required in order to mitigate the risk posed by microbes.

60
00:07:06,030 --> 00:07:11,730
Risk mitigation for serological procedures.

61
00:07:11,730 --> 00:07:17,730
Serological procedures may involve the use of centrifugation for the recovery of serum.

62
00:07:18,730 --> 00:07:26,730
This must be done within a biosafety level 2, DSL2, or a biosafety level 3 laboratory.

63
00:07:26,730 --> 00:07:32,730
Certain procedures such as samples derived from patients infected with risk group 4 pathogens

64
00:07:32,730 --> 00:07:41,730
may need to be done in a BSL4 facility in order to limit the risk of exposure of the laboratory workers

65
00:07:41,730 --> 00:07:50,110
and the environment to the biological agent.

66
00:07:50,110 --> 00:07:59,110
One of the key aspects of sample management involves the processing of waste after the sample has been analyzed.

67
00:07:59,110 --> 00:08:05,110
All laboratory waste derived from the process of clinical samples must be treated as hazardous.

68
00:08:05,110 --> 00:08:09,110
Waste should be labeled appropriately and catalogued.

69
00:08:09,110 --> 00:08:14,110
Waste must be autoclaved except for specific volatile or corrosive substances

70
00:08:14,110 --> 00:08:19,110
which may necessitate special decontamination procedures.

71
00:08:19,110 --> 00:08:24,110
Biological waste may also be incinerated after autoclaving.

72
00:08:24,110 --> 00:08:33,110
Generally, laboratories appoint a specialized company to manage biological waste after decontamination at the facility.

73
00:08:33,110 --> 00:08:38,220
Performance assessment.

74
00:08:38,220 --> 00:08:45,220
Performance can be assessed at the facility by analysis of the incident and accident reports.

75
00:08:46,220 --> 00:08:56,220
Baseline monitoring of serum of the laboratory personnel will also provide an indication of the degree of risk mitigation.

76
00:08:56,220 --> 00:09:04,220
Procedural audits based on observation of the personnel by the biosafety officer or an independent auditor

77
00:09:04,220 --> 00:09:08,220
also provide the relevant assessment of performance.

78
00:09:08,220 --> 00:09:12,650
Reporting of incidents.

79
00:09:12,650 --> 00:09:16,650
All incidents must be reported to the biosafety officer.

80
00:09:16,650 --> 00:09:25,650
Appropriate documentation of the incident in a specific form is necessary in order to ensure traceability.

81
00:09:25,650 --> 00:09:35,650
Identification of the root cause of the incident is essential in order to prevent recurrence of that particular incident.

82
00:09:36,650 --> 00:09:46,440
Revision of the standard operating procedure is required in order to prevent the same incident from recovering.

83
00:09:46,440 --> 00:09:48,440
Reporting of accidents.

84
00:09:48,440 --> 00:09:54,440
Accidents pose a serious threat to personnel as well as the operation of the laboratory facility.

85
00:09:54,440 --> 00:09:58,440
All accidents must be reported to the laboratory management.

86
00:09:58,440 --> 00:10:05,440
The management will be required to report an accident to the local authorities in order to facilitate investigation.

87
00:10:06,440 --> 00:10:17,440
Identification of the causative factors and the controls which can be applied to mitigate the risk of accidents is essential in order to prevent recurrence of these accidents.

88
00:10:17,440 --> 00:10:21,170
Data security.

89
00:10:21,170 --> 00:10:25,170
Clinical samples involve the management of patient data.

90
00:10:25,170 --> 00:10:28,170
All data must be kept confidential.

91
00:10:28,170 --> 00:10:32,170
Employees must be required to sign a non-disclosure agreement.

92
00:10:32,170 --> 00:10:35,170
Data must be secured electronically.

93
00:10:35,170 --> 00:10:40,170
All printed documents must be stored securely as controlled documents.

94
00:10:40,170 --> 00:10:48,170
Copies of documents or data must not be made prior to obtaining written authorization.

95
00:10:48,170 --> 00:10:51,170
Inventory management.

96
00:10:51,170 --> 00:10:55,170
Patient samples must be stored for future detailed analysis.

97
00:10:55,170 --> 00:10:59,170
The samples must be stored under the appropriate conditions.

98
00:10:59,170 --> 00:11:02,170
Physical security must be maintained.

99
00:11:02,170 --> 00:11:09,170
Inventory management is essential in order to ensure traceability of samples.

100
00:11:09,170 --> 00:11:12,170
Compliance with specific standards.

101
00:11:12,170 --> 00:11:18,170
Your respective organization may be required to comply with specific national and local regulations.

102
00:11:18,170 --> 00:11:23,170
Please refer to these laws and regulations when developing your biosafety manual.

103
00:11:24,170 --> 00:11:35,170
A general document for reference is the CWA 15793, also known as the SEND workshop agreement, on laboratory bioresk management.

104
00:11:35,170 --> 00:11:40,170
I will be covering this in another tutorial on laboratory bioresk management.

105
00:11:40,170 --> 00:11:51,020
To summarize, this module was designed and developed to introduce you to the basic guidelines in the management of clinical samples.

106
00:11:51,020 --> 00:12:00,020
You will have to develop specific procedures for bioresk management based on your organizational procedures and in compliance with local standards.

107
00:12:00,020 --> 00:12:05,780
Thank you very much for participating in this module.

108
00:12:05,780 --> 00:12:07,780
Stay biosale!
